    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d91">

                <pb facs="0123" n="111" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg111"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">641.006/547: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-03-19T20:00:00+01:00">March 19, 1940—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 19—3 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>692. Department’s 512, March 18 and Embassy’s 682, March 18, 7 p.m.<anchor n="48"
                        xml:id="d91-f1"/> Further energetic representations have been made in
                    appropriate quarters today, and it is believed that they gained some
                    appreciation of the unfavorable reception recent British import orders have had
                    in the United States. Board of Trade advises tonight that supplementary
                    statement has been prepared for release by Embassy in Washington which they
                    expect to cable immediately.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennedy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
